{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"disputable-duncical-skipper","creation_channel":"cli","description":"Retire the `reconcile` CLI verb and the `--mode` flag from BOTH the CLI wrapper and the engine\nargparse, migrating every enumerated consumer in the same change.\n\nCarved out of `4752-3afb-e8bd-448a`, which now owns only the `bridge` verb-group registration.\n`depends_on 4752` because the replacement surface (`bridge preview` / `bridge sync`) must exist\nbefore the old verb can be removed.\n\n## No alias — the defaults invert\n\n`rebar reconcile` with no arguments means DRY-RUN (the CLI wrapper injects `--mode dry-run` at\n`_cli/__init__.py:145-160`), while `python -m rebar_reconciler` itself defaults to LIVE, and\n`_lib_ops.py:92` defaults to dry-run again — three entry points, two conventions. `bridge sync`\nwith no arguments MUTATES. An alias would therefore be a silent behaviour change: anyone with\n`reconcile` in a script must re-read the line. No alias, no deprecation forwarder.\n\n## Surface scope — CLI + engine ONLY (carried from `4752`)\n\n`reconcile`/`mode` lives on THREE surfaces, not one: the CLI, the public library API\n`rebar.reconcile(mode=...)` (`src/rebar/__init__.py:51` re-exporting `_lib_ops.py:92`), and the MCP\ntool `reconcile(mode=...)` (`_mcp_reads.py:279`, surfaced via `mcp_server.py`). THIS ticket retires\nthe CLI + engine only. The library and MCP retirements belong to sibling `f789-3f9d-8b35-4e0d`,\nwhich ADDS the bridge surface to those same two interfaces — so each surface's old verb is removed\nby the same change that ships its replacement, leaving no window where an MCP or library caller has\nneither. `f789` depends_on this ticket, which enforces that order.\n\n## `mode.py` SURVIVES this ticket — only the FLAG goes (carried from `4752` round-2 B4)\n\nTwo live consumers keep `MODE_CAPS` and the `Mode` rank ladder alive past this ticket:\n* the **MCP surface** gates mutating runs on `MODE_CAPS[parsed] != 0`\n  (`_mcp_reads.py:41,286,291`), and that surface is retired by `f789`, not here;\n* the **phase gate** ranks by `Mode` and — per sibling `1104-93eb-6ce0-4a19`'s decision — its pause\n  compatibility value must remain a REAL `Mode` member (`gated_mode: \"reconcile-check\"`), because a\n  non-`Mode` shim makes `_mode_blocks` swallow the `ValueError` and silently fail OPEN.\nSo `mode.py` survives with its table and ladder intact; its eventual disposition belongs to `f789`\nand `1104`. Deleting it here would break both.\n\n## B3 — The production consumer is enumerated and migrated\n\n`.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml:262` runs `rebar reconcile --mode \"$MODE\"`. This is not an\nincidental caller: `$MODE` is the documented ROLLBACK BRAKE (epic `3006-e198-13db-4e1f`), so\nretiring `--mode` removes an operational safety control unless its replacement is stated. Mapping:\n* `MODE=reconcile-check` / `MODE=dry-run` -> `bridge preview` (no mutation)\n* `MODE=bootstrap-strict` / `bootstrap-throttle` -> `bridge sync --max-changes 10` / `--max-changes 100`\n* `MODE=live` -> `bridge sync` (uncapped)\n* the brake's \"stop entirely\" position is no longer a mode at all — it is `bridge pause` (sibling\n  `1104-93eb-6ce0-4a19`), which is strictly stronger because it is remote-persisted and applies\n  fleet-wide rather than to one `workflow_dispatch` invocation.\nThe `workflow_dispatch` mode input is renamed to the new vocabulary in the SAME change, so the brake\nis never absent. The header comment at `reconcile-bridge.yml:26` (which documents the invocation as\n`rebar reconcile --mode <mode>`) is updated with it.\n\n## B2 — Direct engine callers are enumerated and migrated\n\nFour call sites invoke the engine's `--mode` outside the CLI and break the moment the flag goes:\n`scripts/jira-pressure-test/e2e_validation_probe.sh:85,429` and\n`scripts/jira-pressure-test/e2e_field_validation_probe.sh:143,1787` (via their `run_reconciler`\nhelper), using `bootstrap-throttle` and `reconcile-check`. They migrate in the SAME change:\n`--mode bootstrap-throttle` -> `sync --max-changes 100`, `--mode reconcile-check` -> `preview`.\n\n## B5 — the same scripts also move off `--filter-local-ids`\n\nThis ticket also removes `--filter-local-ids` (replaced by `--only`, which limits what is EXAMINED\nas well as what is changed — unlike `--filter-local-ids`, which filtered only writes and was marked\n\"for validation use only\" for that reason). The two pressure-test scripts above use it too, so\ntheir `--filter-local-ids` invocations are rewritten to `--only` in this same change; leaving them\non a flag this ticket deletes would break them exactly as `--mode` would.\n\n## B3 (tests) — engine `--mode` test consumers MIGRATE, they are not deleted\n\n`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/orchestrate/test_reconcile_main.py` (14 `--mode` occurrences, driving\n`main(['--mode=...'])`) plus `tests/external/live_jira_dc/_dc_support.py` (which also maps its\n`only` argument onto `--filter-local-ids` at line 208), `test_reconcile_pass.py`, and\n`test_user_guide_commands.py`. These MIGRATE rather than being deleted — they are the regression\ncover for the entrypoint this ticket rewrites, so losing them would remove the proof that the new\nsurface behaves like the old one where it should.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The `reconcile` CLI verb no longer dispatches (`_cli/__init__.py:49,146,481` arms removed) and\n      does not appear in `rebar --help`; NO alias and no deprecation forwarder ships\n- [ ] `--mode` is gone from the CLI reconcile wrapper (`_cli/__init__.py:145-160`, including the\n      injected `--mode dry-run` default) and from the engine argparse\n      (`rebar_reconciler/__main__.py`); `--filter-local-ids` is gone with it\n- [ ] `mode.py`'s `MODE_CAPS` table and `Mode` ladder still exist and are still consumed at the end\n      of this ticket; a test asserts the phase gate can still round-trip\n      `gated_mode: \"reconcile-check\"` as a real `Mode` member (B4)\n- [ ] A test asserts the CLI no longer accepts `--mode` or the `reconcile` verb, AND that the\n      library `rebar.reconcile(...)` and the MCP `reconcile` tool are STILL callable at this\n      ticket's boundary — proving the staged retirement left no surface broken mid-epic\n- [ ] `.github/workflows/reconcile-bridge.yml` is migrated off `rebar reconcile --mode \"$MODE\"` at\n      line 262, and its `workflow_dispatch` mode input is renamed to the new vocabulary in the SAME\n      change, so the epic-3006 rollback brake is never absent (B3). The full mode -> new-surface\n      mapping above is what the workflow implements\n- [ ] The four direct engine callers in `scripts/jira-pressure-test/` are migrated in the same\n      change (B2), and their `--filter-local-ids` uses are rewritten to `--only` (B5); a test or CI\n      step proves no script in the tree invokes `--mode` or `--filter-local-ids` after this ticket\n- [ ] The engine `--mode` tests are MIGRATED, not deleted (B3), and still cover the same entrypoint\n      behaviours under the new vocabulary; `_dc_support.py`'s `only` argument maps to `--only`\n- [ ] Every shipped flag's help text contains no internals (no \"mode\", \"rank\", \"gate\", \"lock\",\n      \"cap\", \"pass\", \"binding store\", no exit-code numbers)\n- [ ] Docs are updated: `docs/user-guide.md`, `docs/cli-reference.md` and `docs/jira-sync-setup.md`\n      no longer instruct readers to run `rebar reconcile` or set `mode = ...`; release notes record\n      the retirement and the no-alias decision with its rationale","id":"4a44-4abb-ae91-4e51","parent_id":"2f79-2b32-28c5-4eb3","priority":1,"tags":["bridge","ux"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Retire the reconcile verb and the --mode flag (CLI + engine)"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786095531232798001,"uuid":"a24fc239-d2bd-42b3-b6f7-e813b7c23fe3"}